---
name: fix-task
version: 3.10.0
description: lead fix assignment in atm-bd-orchestration. task_id is a poured fix bead (`<sprint>.<ref>-r<n>-fix`, carrying its blocking finding) or an important or minor finding bead; the fix is a new layer at the top of the stack the finding was found on.
format: xml
required_variables:
  - task_id
  - sprint
  - stack
  - pr_target
  - worktree_path
  - branch
  - primary_checkout
  - phase_root
  - test_command
  - policy_path
  - requirements_globs
  - adr_globs
metadata:
  type: fix-task
  tags:
    - stage:fix
    - orchestration:beads
  workflow:
    scope:
      kind: sprint
      variable: sprint
    state: fix-start
    stage: fix
    transition: start
---
<atm-task id="{{ task_id }}" sprint="{{ sprint }}" mode="fix">
  <description><![CDATA[Fix `{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` (found in sprint {{ sprint | string | cdata_escape }}). `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` is the assignment: a poured fix bead carries the finding in its description and `metadata.remedy`; a finding bead has the finding and its evidence in its description, the remedy in its design and when it is fixed in its acceptance criteria.]]></description>

  <worktree><![CDATA[{{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}]]></worktree>
  <branch><![CDATA[{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}]]></branch>
  <stack><![CDATA[{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}]]></stack>
  <pr-target><![CDATA[{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}]]></pr-target>
  <stack-discipline><![CDATA[The finding was found on a frozen layer. `{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` is a new layer cut from `origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}`, the top of stack `{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}` at dispatch; its PR lands on the stack's current top. Work only on this branch; the task assigner is the stack writer and you never run `gh stack link`, `sync`, `rebase` or `merge`.]]></stack-discipline>

  <workflow>
    <step id="a"><![CDATA[Before claiming, run `git fetch origin` in the worktree (no rebase: the branch was cut from the stack top, and check (6) below, `--is-ancestor`, proves it holds `origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}`), then `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/assignment-gates.py dev --root {{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }} --bead {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --pr-target {{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }} --worktree {{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}` from the worktree (scripts come from the primary checkout, never the branch). Each check, its command and its refusal code: (1) plan valid: `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-beads/scripts/validate-plan --root {{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }}` exits 0, else `PLAN_INVALID`; (2) ready: `bd ready -n 0 --json` lists the bead, else `NOT_READY`; (3) claimable without `--force`: `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --json` shows status `open` and assignee empty or `$ATM_IDENTITY`, else `UNCLAIMABLE`; (4) target: `metadata.pr_target` is `{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` or `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/<metadata.pr_target> origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` exits 0 (`pr_target` is a lower bound), else `PR_TARGET_MISMATCH`; (5) difficulty: your `atm members --json` model is in `DIFFICULTY_MODELS[metadata.difficulty]`, else `DIFFICULTY_MISMATCH`; (6) base: `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }} HEAD` exits 0, else `WRONG_BASE`. `GATE_CANNOT_RUN` when a command fails. Reuse an existing workflow class bead for the same failure signature: append the task id, head, command and failure evidence, and cite the class id in the refusal. If no class matches, report the signature to the task assigner for classification and cite that message instead; do not create a per-task shadow or delay the refusal. Otherwise claim with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --claim --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"`, then start `atm task start {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} "<one line: what you will do first>"`.]]></step>
    <step id="a1"><![CDATA[When it prints `NOT_READY` the bead is not ready: do not claim it and do not start the task. Find the root cause: `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` (status, assignee), `bd blocked --json | jq --arg b {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} '.[] | select(.id == $b)'` (open blockers: another fix that must land first), and `bd show <blocker>` for each. Report it to the task assigner: the bead, why it is not ready, and which bead or agent has to move. Then wait.]]></step>
    <step id="b"><![CDATA[Read `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}`, then its `metadata.sprint_bead` for the scope it belongs to. Read every id in its own `metadata.requirements` and `metadata.adrs` (requirements: {{ requirements_globs | join(", ") | string | cdata_escape }}; ADRs: {{ adr_globs | join(", ") | string | cdata_escape }}): the fix must keep satisfying them. `metadata.sprint_bead` is the sprint whose contract they come from. Read the repository QA policy `{{ policy_path | string | cdata_escape }}` before editing, and every guideline it names for the languages this fix touches.]]></step>
    <step id="c"><![CDATA[Before fixing, verify the defect is still present at the cited file:line in this worktree. If it does not reproduce (already fixed, superseded, or the code path no longer exists), do no speculative or invented work in its name: close it as not reproducible (step f) with the file:line you checked.]]></step>
    <step id="d"><![CDATA[Read the complete finding and remedy and enumerate all tasks that must be completed; create an itemized private checklist outside the tracked tree with every task identified, and work through the checklist one item at a time. Fix every occurrence in scope, not just the first: when the finding is a repeatable pattern, search the worktree for the same pattern and fix each one. Touch only the files the remedy needs. Then go through the checklist again one item at a time and confirm each is done. Run `{{ test_command | string | cdata_escape }}` until it passes with zero failures.]]></step>
    <step id="d1"><![CDATA[If the work needs a change other live branches need at the same time (a shared type or trait signature, or a critical bug every branch carries), stop that edit and tell the task assigner the exact change and the branches it breaks; follow the assigner's base (`.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/SKILL.md`, Parallel Quick Fix) and report a quick-fix PR you open by plain `atm send`.]]></step>
    <step id="e"><![CDATA[Rebase onto the stack's current top (it may have moved since dispatch): `git fetch origin`, the top is what `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/assignment-gates.py stack-top --pr-target {{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` prints on exit 0 (from GitHub's stacks); any other exit prints a code (`STACK_AMBIGUOUS`, `GATE_CANNOT_RUN`): never guess, refuse with it by step f1; then `git rebase origin/<top>`, re-run `{{ test_command | string | cdata_escape }}`, then `git push --force-with-lease origin {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}`. Then open the PR against that top: `gh pr create --base <top> --head {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }} --fill` (never `--draft`), run `/sc-gh-stack-view` (read-only) and keep its output verbatim. Record the full head SHA, the top as `rebased_onto`, and the PR number and url.]]></step>
    <step id="f"><![CDATA[Close the bead and the task together, `bd close` first; close the task only if `bd close` succeeded: `bd close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --reason "fixed at <short sha>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"` (or `--reason "not reproducible: <file:line checked>"`) and `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} completed --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/fix-complete.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/fix-complete-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`. Build the vars from that template's `required_variables`: `task_id` and `sprint` unchanged, `commit` the pushed head, `rebased_onto` the top, `pr_number` and `pr_url` from step e and `stack_view` the verbatim `/sc-gh-stack-view` output (omit all five for `not_reproducible`), the outcome, and the gate output lines. Write the vars file outside the repository. The fix then gets its own sanity check and QA (for a poured fix bead, the group's sanity and qa beads). Only this close frees your queue.]]></step>
    <step id="f1"><![CDATA[If the fix cannot be done at all, reuse an existing workflow class bead for the same failure signature: append the task id, head, command and failure evidence, and cite the class id in the refusal. If no class matches, report the signature to the task assigner for classification and cite that message instead; do not create a per-task shadow or delay the refusal. A serious infrastructure failure is also announced once per cause to the oversight recipients (`.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/SKILL.md` Lead Role). Leave the bead open with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --status open --assignee "" --append-notes "FIX_CANNOT_COMPLETE; workflow class or escalation <reference>: <reason>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"` and close the task with `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} refused --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/task-refused.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/refused-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`. A fix or finding bead is never closed unfixed by the fixer; only quality-mgr (ceremony) or the task assigner closes one without a fix.]]></step>
    <step id="g"><![CDATA[After the close, read ATM again: the next queued task starts only now.]]></step>
  </workflow>
</atm-task>
